// # Coral Build Cache — Full Spec

// ## 0. What the cache must answer

// For every module, one question: **"Is the compiled output still valid?"**

// Valid means:

// - Every source file in the module is byte-identical to last build.
// - Every `lib.crl` re-export line is unchanged.
// - Every dependency's compiled output is unchanged.
// - Compiler version, build flags, and target are unchanged.

// If all four hold → reuse the cached object. If any fails → recompile.

// ---

// ## 1. Layout on disk

// ```
// .coral/
// ├── modules.map                    # generated, always fresh
// ├── cache/
// │   ├── v1/                        # cache format version
// │   │   ├── coral-common/
// │   │   │   ├── meta                 # metadata (text)
// │   │   │   ├── fingerprint          # hash (short text)
// │   │   │   ├── lib.crlo             # compiled object
// │   │   │   └── deps                 # list of dep folders + their fingerprints
// │   │   ├── coral-lexer/
// │   │   │   ├── meta
// │   │   │   ├── fingerprint
// │   │   │   ├── lib.crlo
// │   │   │   └── deps
// │   │   └── ...
// │   ├── sources/                   # per-file content hashes
// │   │   ├── ab/abcdef...           # content-addressed, 2-char shard
// │   │   └── ...
// │   └── index                      # summary: module → fingerprint
// └── graph.dot
// ```

// Rules:

// - `v1/` is the **cache format version*-> Bump it if the hashing scheme or metadata shape changes. Old versions are ignored, not migrated.
// - Module folders use the **raw folder name** (`coral-common`), not the alias. Aliases are a source-level convenience; the cache is keyed by reality.
// - Everything under `cache/` is disposable. `crl clean` wipes it. Nothing there is source-of-truth.

// ---

// ## 2. What gets hashed

// Four inputs, combined into one fingerprint per module.

// ### 2.1 Source files

// For every `.crl` file **directly in the module folder** (not in subfolders — those are their own modules, or skipped):

// - Hash file content with **BLAKE3** (fast, modern, 256-bit output; SHA-256 is fine too).
// - Include the **relative path** in the hash so a rename counts as a change.
// - Sort files by path before combining so iteration order doesn't matter.

// ```
// sources_hash(module) = BLAKE3(
//     for each .crl file sorted by relpath:
//         relpath || 0x00 || file_size || 0x00 || BLAKE3(file_bytes) || 0x00
// )
// ```

// `lib.crl` is included in this set — it's a `.crl` file.

// **Why content hash and not mtime/size:** mtime lies (touch, git checkout, clock skew). Content hash never lies. Slower on cold cache, but you already dedupe by size first if you want.

// ### 2.2 Re-export surface

// `lib.crl` is included above, but the **`pub mod` lines** matter to *dependents* even when the file body changes. So extract them separately:

// ```
// surface_hash(module) = BLAKE3(
//     for each `pub mod NAME = import "PATH";` line, in file order:
//         NAME || 0x00 || PATH || 0x00
// )
// ```

// Why separate: if only a function body in `lib.crl` changes, dependents don't need to recompile — the surface they bind against is the same. If a `pub mod` line is added/removed/renamed, dependents *do* recompile. This is the incremental-build win.

// ### 2.3 Dependency fingerprints

// ```
// deps_hash(module) = BLAKE3(
//     for each dep folder, sorted by folder name:
//         dep_folder_name || 0x00 || fingerprint(dep) || 0x00
// )
// ```

// This is what cascades. If `coral-common` changes, its fingerprint changes, so `deps_hash(coral-lexer)` changes, so `coral-lexer` recompiles.

// ### 2.4 Build context

// Things that invalidate everything if they change:

// ```
// context_hash = BLAKE3(
//     compiler_version   || 0x00 ||
//     target             || 0x00 ||
//     opt_level          || 0x00 ||
//     flags(module)      || 0x00 ||
//     cache_format_version
// )
// ```

// Per-module flags (from `override` blocks) go here, not global flags only, so an override on one module doesn't invalidate all.

// ### 2.5 Final fingerprint

// ```
// fingerprint(module) = BLAKE3(
//     "coral-fp-v1"          || 0x00 ||
//     sources_hash(module)   || 0x00 ||
//     surface_hash(module)   || 0x00 ||
//     deps_hash(module)      || 0x00 ||
//     context_hash
// )
// ```

// Hex-encoded, truncated to 32 chars for display, full 64 chars stored.

// ---

// ## 3. Metadata file format

// Each module folder in the cache has a `meta` file. **Text, line-oriented, diffable.** Not JSON — you don't want a parser dependency for something this simple.

// ```
// # coral cache meta v1
// module       = coral-lexer
// fingerprint  = 7f3a91c2e8b45d6a0f1e2d3c4b5a6978
// built_at     = 2026-09-20T14:32:11Z
// compiler     = coralc 0.1.0
// target       = native-x86_64
// opt          = release

// sources_hash = a1b2c3d4e5f60718293a4b5c6d7e8f90
// surface_hash = 0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
// deps_hash    = fedcba9876543210fedcba9876543210
// context_hash = 11112222333344445555666677778888

// sources:
//   lib.crl      = 3a4b5c6d7e8f9012...
//   base.crl     = 9f8e7d6c5b4a3921...
//   escape.crl   = 1a2b3c4d5e6f7081...
//   ident.crl    = aabbccddeeff0011...
//   lexer.crl    = 2233445566778899...
//   number.crl   = 9988776655443322...
//   operator.crl = 1122334455667788...
//   skip_ws.crl  = abcdef0123456789...
//   string.crl   = 0f1e2d3c4b5a6978...
//   table.crl    = 8877665544332211...

// deps:
//   coral-common = 4e3d2c1b0a99887766554433221100ff
//   coral-ast    = 5f4e3d2c1b0a99887766554433221100
// ```

// Key ordering is fixed so diffs are clean. Unknown keys are ignored (forward-compat). Missing required keys → cache miss, recompile.

// ---

// ## 4. Index file

// `.coral/cache/v1/index` — flat summary the builder reads first, one line per module:

// ```
// # coral cache index v1
// coral-ast           7f3a91c2e8b45d6a0f1e2d3c4b5a6978
// coral-backend       3a4b5c6d7e8f9012a1b2c3d4e5f60718
// coral-common        0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef
// coral-lexer         fedcba9876543210fedcba9876543210
// ...
// ```

// Purpose: O(1) lookup of "does this module need recompiling" without stat-ing every `meta` file. If the index is corrupt or missing, fall back to reading each `meta` (slower but correct).

// The index is **derived**, never authoritative. If it disagrees with `meta`, trust `meta` and rewrite the index.

// ---

// ## 5. Source content store

// `.coral/cache/sources/` — a content-addressed store for individual `.crl` file hashes, sharded two hex chars deep:

// ```
// sources/
// ├── 3a/3a4b5c6d7e8f9012a1b2c3d4e5f60718...
// ├── 9f/9f8e7d6c5b4a3921fedcba9876543210...
// ├── 1a/1a2b3c4d5e6f7081aabbccddeeff0011...
// └── ...
// ```

// Each file is **empty** — the *name is the hash-> This exists purely to dedupe: if 200 modules import the same header-like file, the content is hashed once.

// Optional. If you don't care about dedup, drop it. The `meta` file already records per-file hashes.

// ---

// ## 6. Build flow with cache

// ```
// for m in topo_order:
//     # 1. cheap checks first
//     if not exists(cache/v1/<m>/meta):
//         compile(m); continue

//     meta = read_meta(m)

//     # 2. context check (version, target, flags)
//     if context_hash(m) != meta.context_hash:
//         compile(m); continue

//     # 3. source check (content hash per file)
//     cur_sources = hash_sources(m)
//     if cur_sources != meta.sources_hash:
//         compile(m); continue

//     # 4. surface check (pub mod lines)
//     cur_surface = hash_surface(m)
//     if cur_surface != meta.surface_hash:
//         compile(m); continue

//     # 5. deps check (recursive, but memoized)
//     cur_deps = hash_deps(m)
//     if cur_deps != meta.deps_hash:
//         compile(m); continue

//     # 6. all checks passed → skip
//     log("up-to-date", m)
// ```

// Notice the ordering: **cheap checks first*-> Context is a few bytes. Source hashes require reading files (but files are usually in OS page cache). Surface requires parsing `lib.crl`. Deps require recursing, but memoized so each module is hashed once.

// If any check fails, we don't bother with the rest — recompile immediately.

// ---

// ## 7. Incremental correctness — the tricky bits

// ### 7.1 Adding a new dep

// Before: `coral-lexer → coral-common`. After: `coral-lexer → coral-common, coral-ast`.

// `deps_hash(coral-lexer)` changes because the dep set changed. Recompile. Dependents of `coral-lexer` also recompile because its fingerprint changes. Correct.

// ### 7.2 Removing a dep

// Same — dep set changes, recompile.

// ### 7.3 Reordering `pub mod` lines

// Surface hash changes (file order matters). Dependents recompile. This is slightly conservative — reordering doesn't change semantics — but it's cheap and safe. If it bothers you, sort before hashing.

// ### 7.4 Renaming an alias in the build file

// Aliases live in `modules.map`, not in module fingerprints. So renaming `lexer → coral-lexer` to `lx → coral-lexer` does **not** invalidate any module. But it *does* change how source files write imports, so their text changes → their source hash changes → they recompile.

// Wait — that's wrong. The alias rename happens in the build file. The source files still say `import lexer::table`. If you rename the alias, you must edit every source file that used it. So either:

// - The build file is a *global* input to `context_hash` — but then renaming an alias invalidates everything, which is overkill.
// - Or: the alias map is a *separate* fingerprint that modules opt into based on whether they use aliased imports.

// I'd go with: **aliases are part of `modules.map`, and `modules.map`'s hash is part of `context_hash` only for modules that use any aliased import.** If a module only uses raw folder imports or path imports, alias churn doesn't touch it.

// Simpler alternative: **alias renames are treated as source changes.** The builder rewrites source files during `crl map`, updating the imports. Source hash changes → recompile. Done. Pick this one for v1.

// ### 7.5 Compiler version bump

// `compiler_version` is in `context_hash`. Bump compiler → all context hashes change → full rebuild. Correct, unavoidable.

// ### 7.6 Touching a file without content change

// `touch lexer.crl` changes mtime, not content. Since we hash content, nothing happens. Correct.

// ### 7.7 Renaming a source file

// Relative path is in the source hash. Rename → path changes → hash changes → recompile. Correct.

// ### 7.8 Deleting a dep module

// The build fails earlier (E1005 unresolved import) before we get to the cache. Good.

// ### 7.9 Corruption

// If `meta` is unparseable or missing required keys → treat as cache miss. Recompile. Never trust a partial `meta`.

// ---

// ## 8. CLI surface

// ```
// crl build                    # use cache
// crl build --force            # ignore cache, recompile all, rewrite cache
// crl build --explain          # print why each module rebuilt
// crl build --no-cache         # don't write cache (CI dry run)

// crl cache stat               # show cache size, module count, hit rate
// crl cache why <module>       # show which input changed vs last build
// crl cache verify             # rehash everything, report drift
// crl cache gc                 # drop entries not referenced by any module
// crl clean                    # nuke cache/ and build/
// ```

// `crl build --explain` output:

// ```
// coral-common    recompile   sources_hash changed
// coral-lexer     recompile   deps_hash changed (coral-common)
// coral-parser    up-to-date
// coral-frontend  recompile   context_hash changed (flags)
// ```

// `crl cache why coral-lexer`:

// ```
// coral-lexer

// fingerprint  expected  7f3a91c2e8b45d6a0f1e2d3c4b5a6978
//              cached    3a4b5c6d7e8f9012a1b2c3d4e5f60718

// differing input:
//   deps_hash
//     expected  4e3d2c1b0a99887766554433221100ff
//     cached    fedcba9876543210fedcba9876543210
//     reason    coral-common fingerprint changed

// coral-common  4e3d2c1b0a99887766554433221100ff  (was 3a4b...)
//   differing input: sources_hash
//     token.crl changed
// ```

// ---

// ## 9. Concurrent safety

// Two `crl build` runs at once, or one running while an editor triggers another:

// - **Lock file**: `.coral/cache/v1/.lock` — advisory lock, held for the whole build.
// - If lock is held: wait up to N seconds, then error.
// - Cache writes are **atomic**: write to `meta.tmp`, `fsync`, `rename` over `meta`. Rename is atomic on every sane filesystem.
// - Readers never see partial files.
// - A stale lock from a killed process is detected by PID + start time in the lock file; `crl cache gc --break-lock` clears it.

// ---

// ## 10. Cache format versioning

// `cache/v1/` — the `v1` is the format version. Bump when:

// - Hash algorithm changes
// - `meta` schema changes incompatibly
// - Fingerprint input set changes

// Old versions are left alone. New builder uses new dir. `crl cache gc` removes dirs not matching current version.

// Put a `VERSION` file inside `v1/`:

// ```
// coral cache format 1
// hashing: blake3
// fields: sources_hash surface_hash deps_hash context_hash
// ```

// Read it. If missing or mismatched → treat cache as cold.

// ---

// ## 11. Summary table

// | Aspect | Choice |
// |---|---|
// | Hash function | BLAKE3 (fallback SHA-256) |
// | Fingerprint inputs | sources + surface + deps + context |
// | Source hashing | Content, not mtime |
// | Surface separate from body | Yes — for incremental precision |
// | Dep cascade | Via dep fingerprints, memoized |
// | Metadata format | Line-oriented text (`meta`) |
// | Index | Flat text summary, derived |
// | Content store | Optional, sharded by hash |
// | Atomicity | tmp + rename |
// | Concurrency | Advisory lock file |
// | Format version | `cache/v1/`, bump on schema change |
// | Skip logic | Cheap checks first, bail early |
// | Cache key | Raw folder name, not alias |
// | Alias churn | Treated as source change via `crl map` rewrite |

// ---

